<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cape-verdean-creole">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Verdean Creole</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kriolu, Kabuverdianu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape Verdean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a>, known as Kriolu, is a Portuguese-based creole language spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> and by its emigrant communities. It arose from contact between Portuguese settlers and enslaved West Africans on the islands from the late 15th century, making it one of the oldest living creoles. It is the everyday language of almost all Cape Verdeans and the language of music such as morna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-verdean-creole.jpg" alt="Cape Verdean Creole" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cayambe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cidade_Velha_info_panel_UNESCO_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early history of Cape Verdean Creole is hard to trace because of a lack of written documents and because the language was shunned under Portuguese administration. Scholars hold three theories of its formation. One holds that the Portuguese simplified their own language to make it accessible to enslaved African people, a view associated with Prudent, Waldman, Chaudenson and Lopes da Silva. A second, put by Adam and Quint, holds that enslaved African people built it on the grammar of West African languages and replaced the African vocabulary with Portuguese words.</p><p>A third, from linguists such as Chomsky and Bickerton, holds that it arose spontaneously among the population born on the islands, through universal grammar. Its phonology comes mainly from Portuguese of the 15th to 17th centuries, and it keeps some consonants that today's Portuguese has lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Between 90 and 95 per cent of its words come from Portuguese, and the rest from West African languages such as Mandingo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, Temne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balanta">Balanta</a> and Mandjak. Its grammar, however, is very different from Portuguese, and resembles that of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a>. The basic order is subject, verb, object. Verbs have only two forms, the same for every person, and tense, mood and aspect are shown by small particles.</p><p>There are no definite articles, only demonstratives, and only nouns for people and animals have gender. Double and even triple negation is possible. Each of the nine inhabited islands has its own variant, grouped into the southern Sotavento creoles, which include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and Fogo, and the northern Barlavento creoles, which include São Vicente and Santo Antão.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Almost all Cape Verdeans learn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> as their first language, but Portuguese remains the official language and is used in schools, administration and foreign relations. The two live in a state of diglossia, and speakers switch between them in informal talk. The only writing system recognised by the authorities is the Unified Alphabet for the Writing of the Cape Verdean Language, ALUPEC, approved on an experimental basis by Decree-Law No.</p><p>67/98 in 1998 and institutionalised by Decree-Law No. 8/2009 in 2009. It is neither required nor always used, and the law allows other systematic models. Writers have long used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> for literature and song: Eugénio Tavares wrote poems in the Brava variant, and the lyrics of Nôs Ráça by Manuel d'Novas are in São Vicente Creole.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cape Verdean Creole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its name is the <b>Cape Verdean Creole</b> form of the Portuguese word saudade, a longing for what is absent.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sodade">Sodade</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese settlers and enslaved Africans formed a Creole society with its own language, <b>Kriolu</b>, and music.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-cape-verde">Portuguese Cape Verde</a></li></ul></section>
